[{:db/id 17592186147451 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-071695a5-71a8-5be3-9519-3660055fc2db" "question-f3e9f427-a3b5-5de3-bad3-b21c91d9d341"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-071695a5-71a8-5be3-9519-3660055fc2db" :question/id #uuid "071695a5-71a8-5be3-9519-3660055fc2db" :question/math-academy-id "q-94301" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=17{e}^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is an exponential force. The constant factor in the exponent is $1,$ which is not a root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular exponential solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)={e}^{t},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=-1,\\,B=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\pi -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi +{e}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =({e}^{\\pi }-1)\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aa0a7def-a246-5553-afa4-48288b187ed4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aa0a7def-a246-5553-afa4-48288b187ed4" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa0a7def-a246-5553-afa4-48288b187ed4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ebb0d34a-111a-5f03-a468-2ba62a9a6c8f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a2ef1d89-d277-5de1-9f67-b528494c18f1" "answer-ec44def9-9e39-53ed-aff9-0f399cc5b8fb" "answer-290d0431-34bf-5241-9afa-d9a916322c31" "answer-ebb0d34a-111a-5f03-a468-2ba62a9a6c8f" "answer-36cbb06e-148b-54b4-91d4-a81f3e7aa5fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2ef1d89-d277-5de1-9f67-b528494c18f1" :answer/id #uuid "a2ef1d89-d277-5de1-9f67-b528494c18f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(\\pi )=(2{e}^{2\\pi }-\\frac{1}{4})\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec44def9-9e39-53ed-aff9-0f399cc5b8fb" :answer/id #uuid "ec44def9-9e39-53ed-aff9-0f399cc5b8fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(\\pi )=(2{e}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2})\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-290d0431-34bf-5241-9afa-d9a916322c31" :answer/id #uuid "290d0431-34bf-5241-9afa-d9a916322c31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(\\pi )=({e}^{2\\pi }+1)\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebb0d34a-111a-5f03-a468-2ba62a9a6c8f" :answer/id #uuid "ebb0d34a-111a-5f03-a468-2ba62a9a6c8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(\\pi )=({e}^{\\pi }-1)\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36cbb06e-148b-54b4-91d4-a81f3e7aa5fd" :answer/id #uuid "36cbb06e-148b-54b4-91d4-a81f3e7aa5fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(\\pi )=({e}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{4})\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f3e9f427-a3b5-5de3-bad3-b21c91d9d341" :question/id #uuid "f3e9f427-a3b5-5de3-bad3-b21c91d9d341" :question/math-academy-id "q-94309" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in centimeters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+36y=18t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $1\\,\\text{cm/s},$ find the position of the particle after $2$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=36\\,⟹\\,\\omega =6.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a linear polynomial. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a linear polynomial, i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha +\\beta t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,\\beta $ are constants to be determined.\n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =0,\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{t}{2},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t+\\frac{t}{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $1\\,\\text{cm/s}$, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=1.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=0,\\,B=\\frac{1}{12}\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t}{12}+\\frac{t}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $2$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡6(2)}{12}+\\frac{(2)}{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡12}{12}+1)\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-97ee9863-7827-5ffb-961e-95840bc8f69d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-97ee9863-7827-5ffb-961e-95840bc8f69d" :answer-field/id #uuid "97ee9863-7827-5ffb-961e-95840bc8f69d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3aa62a3b-1769-5d05-86db-8812b1d7f0d2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4e1b4f6f-1427-536a-99b9-724dc4e64da2" "answer-41ac66cb-2440-5faa-9ffe-032ed8000f79" "answer-3aa62a3b-1769-5d05-86db-8812b1d7f0d2" "answer-c8676819-95da-5da8-9760-80284b82e282" "answer-2637def5-3485-53ec-a92f-f5dafce114a1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e1b4f6f-1427-536a-99b9-724dc4e64da2" :answer/id #uuid "4e1b4f6f-1427-536a-99b9-724dc4e64da2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(2)=(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡12}{6}+1)\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41ac66cb-2440-5faa-9ffe-032ed8000f79" :answer/id #uuid "41ac66cb-2440-5faa-9ffe-032ed8000f79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(2)=(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡6}{6}+2)\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3aa62a3b-1769-5d05-86db-8812b1d7f0d2" :answer/id #uuid "3aa62a3b-1769-5d05-86db-8812b1d7f0d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(2)=(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡12}{12}+1)\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8676819-95da-5da8-9760-80284b82e282" :answer/id #uuid "c8676819-95da-5da8-9760-80284b82e282" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(2)=(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡12}{6}-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2637def5-3485-53ec-a92f-f5dafce114a1" :answer/id #uuid "2637def5-3485-53ec-a92f-f5dafce114a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(2)=(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡6}{12}+\\frac{1}{2})\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186357739 :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(25t+ϕ)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$ It can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\omega =5.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =0.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nOnly III matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-5802b41f-ba3b-55d7-ba60-f9644f140c8f" :activity/id #uuid "5802b41f-ba3b-55d7-ba60-f9644f140c8f" :activity/title "Forced Oscillators" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048622 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-f76f52bb-0a3a-5b73-9f35-a1138cfc5f7b" "step-84c18301-1b51-5f16-bb6b-0040c628eb02" "step-d264594d-e99c-51f2-ad2d-ef5158a638af" "step-5a511ebc-694e-548d-808d-9bde7882c044" "step-b7e52d0a-b5e2-5243-aece-1bf9c6297d76"] :activity/first-step "step-f76f52bb-0a3a-5b73-9f35-a1138cfc5f7b" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-f76f52bb-0a3a-5b73-9f35-a1138cfc5f7b" :step/id #uuid "f76f52bb-0a3a-5b73-9f35-a1138cfc5f7b" :step/content 17592186335722 :step/next "step-84c18301-1b51-5f16-bb6b-0040c628eb02" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-84c18301-1b51-5f16-bb6b-0040c628eb02" :step/id #uuid "84c18301-1b51-5f16-bb6b-0040c628eb02" :step/content 17592186335723 :step/next "step-d264594d-e99c-51f2-ad2d-ef5158a638af" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d264594d-e99c-51f2-ad2d-ef5158a638af" :step/id #uuid "d264594d-e99c-51f2-ad2d-ef5158a638af" :step/content "question-071695a5-71a8-5be3-9519-3660055fc2db" :step/next "step-5a511ebc-694e-548d-808d-9bde7882c044" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5a511ebc-694e-548d-808d-9bde7882c044" :step/id #uuid "5a511ebc-694e-548d-808d-9bde7882c044" :step/content "question-f3e9f427-a3b5-5de3-bad3-b21c91d9d341" :step/next "step-b7e52d0a-b5e2-5243-aece-1bf9c6297d76" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b7e52d0a-b5e2-5243-aece-1bf9c6297d76" :step/id #uuid "b7e52d0a-b5e2-5243-aece-1bf9c6297d76" :step/content 17592186357739 :db/ensure [:step/validate]}]
